From 921cde8fbe857f1bb216756a12f13f48db69fbac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:35:35 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] Servers: pass the block hash to getrawtransaction for an
 indexed transaction

blockchain.transaction.get forwarded getrawtransaction <txid> <verbose>
with no block hash. For a confirmed transaction bitcoind can answer that
only with -txindex, and a pruned node cannot have -txindex (Core and Knots
refuse the combination), so every confirmed lookup failed against one.

Fulcrum already knows where each transaction is: txhash2txnum gives its
height, and the header gives the block hash, the lookup behind
blockchain.transaction.get_confirmed_blockhash. Do that lookup in a worker
thread, then pass the hash. bitcoind then needs only the block, which
btc-rpc-proxy fetches from peers when the node has pruned it (proxy
v0.8.0 and later). The hash comes from BTC::HeaderHashRev, so a BLAKE2b
block is named by its BLAKE2b id.

A transaction not in the index (unconfirmed, or unknown) is still asked
for without a hash, which finds it in the mempool. When that fails, the
node answers -5 with advice to enable -txindex; replace the message with
one that says the transaction is not in this server's index, keeping the
code and the ElectrumX-style formatting.

The bitcoind request is counted against the client's throttle up front,
on the client thread, as the request is read. Counted only when it
reached bitcoind, after the lookup on the thread pool, the throttle bit
too late: one client pipelined tens of thousands of lookups (each a block
fetched from peers on a pruned node) before its reads were paused, where
every other bitcoind-backed method stops at 50. generic_async_to_bitcoind
gains an alreadyCounted flag; the throttle block moved, unchanged, into
bdThrottleCount. The lookup never throws, so the count is always undone.

The reply is relayed only if it is the transaction asked for (txid of the
raw hex, or the verbose txid): with a block hash it can come from a block
fetched from peers through a proxy.

A lookup that names a block gets the operator's bitcoind_timeout rather
than the fixed 15 s Fulcrum gives every client-originated request: on a
pruned node the block may be fetched from peers, which can take longer
(up to 40 s measured on a live server with Tor and I2P peers). Shown on
regtest with a relay holding each such request for 20 s.
